// Title: Potential Unquoted Service Path Reconnaissance Via Wmic.EXE
// Sigma ID: 68bcd73b-37ef-49cb-95fc-edc809730be6
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2022-06-20 | Modified: 2023-09-11
// ATT&CK: execution, T1047
// Description: Detects known WMI recon method to look for unquoted service paths using wmic. Often used by
//   pentester and attacker enumeration scripts
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://github.com/nccgroup/redsnarf/blob/35949b30106ae543dc6f2bc3f1be10c6d9a8d40e/redsnarf.py
//   - https://github.com/S3cur3Th1sSh1t/Creds/blob/eac23d67f7f90c7fc8e3130587d86158c22aa398/PowershellScripts/jaws-enum.ps1
//   - https://www.absolomb.com/2018-01-26-Windows-Privilege-Escalation-Guide/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_wmic_recon_unquoted_service_search.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "wmic.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\WMIC.exe") and (ProcessCommandLine contains " service get " and ProcessCommandLine contains "name,displayname,pathname,startmode")
